## Context

Review feedback noted the split heuristic falls back to count-midpoint when the byte-weighted split lands on the first item. For "whale-file" workloads (one file dominates byte volume), this means the dominant file is grouped with half the remaining files rather than isolated for parallel processing.

## Current State

```rust
// crates/gossip-connectors/src/filesystem.rs:615-625 — byte_weighted_split_idx
let split_idx = if total == 0 {
    start_idx + count / 2  // zero-size fallback: count-balanced
} else {
    let target = base.saturating_add(total / 2);
    let relative = self.prefix_sums[start_idx..range_end].partition_point(|&s| s < target);
    let idx = start_idx + relative;
    if idx == start_idx {
        start_idx + count / 2  // whale-at-start fallback: count-balanced
    } else {
        idx
    }
};
```

The same logic exists in `common.rs:569-608` (`choose_split_index`).

```rust
// crates/gossip-connectors/src/common.rs:598-607 — linear-scan equivalent
if idx == start_idx {
    start_idx + (range_end - start_idx) / 2  // same count-balanced fallback
}
```

## Desired State

When byte-weighted split lands on the first item, instead of falling back to count-midpoint, evaluate a small set of candidates (e.g., `idx`, `idx+1`, clamped) and pick the one that minimizes `max(bytes_left, bytes_right)`. This isolates the whale for parallel scheduling.

Use existing prefix sums for O(1) evaluation of each candidate.

