### Design Notes

**Why not a single combined score?** OWASP Risk Rating explicitly warns against collapsing likelihood × impact into a single number. A combined score of "5" could mean "definitely real, low danger" (auto-close) or "maybe real, catastrophic danger" (escalate immediately). These demand completely different response actions. Keeping both dimensions visible preserves actionability.

**Why configurable?** Different organizations have dramatically different risk appetites. A regulated financial institution processing PCI data has zero tolerance for missed findings. A fast-moving startup may prefer aggressive noise reduction. The thresholds should be tunable without code changes.

**Why off by default?** The triage layer is a lossy filter — it suppresses findings below the confidence gate. Until users have confidence in the threat scoring model's calibration, suppression should be opt-in. Default behavior: surface all findings, sorted by priority_rank.

