## Context

This is Phase 2.1 of the bounded-memory filesystem connector epic (gossip-8936). The filesystem hierarchy provides free key-range partitioning: all entries under a common directory prefix form a contiguous range in the sorted keyspace. A shard with key range `[src/m, src/z)` never contains any file under `docs/` because every key prefixed by `docs/` is lexicographically outside `[src/m, src/z)`. Today, the walker traverses the entire directory tree regardless of shard bounds, collecting entries that are later filtered during pagination via binary search (`resolve_bounds` in `common.rs:122-140`). For a 100-shard deployment over a 10M-file tree, each worker redundantly walks all 10M files and discards ~99% of them.

Key-range subtree pruning exploits the prefix-contiguity property to skip entire directory subtrees during the walk, reducing per-worker memory from O(N) to O(N/shards) and cutting I/O proportionally. This is the same principle behind Bigtable tablet locality and CockroachDB range-scoped compaction: restrict I/O to the data your shard actually owns.

**Research finding (F5/F6 from epic):** Filesystem hierarchy as free partitioning — all entries under a common prefix form contiguous key ranges. Per-directory sorted DFS (Phase 1.1) provides the prefix-ordered traversal needed for correct pruning.

## Current State

### Walk function accepts no shard bounds (`filesystem.rs:876-884`)

```rust
// crates/gossip-connectors/src/filesystem.rs:876-884
fn walk_dir_collect_files(
    root: &Path,
    max_depth: usize,
    deadline: Option<Instant>,
    max_warnings: usize,
    overflow_count: &mut usize,
    out: &mut Vec<FileEntry>,
    warnings: &mut Vec<WalkWarning>,
