## Context

The commit pipeline worker handles the result of `CheckpointAggregatorInput::new()`
after a successful `commit_translation()`. When `CheckpointAggregatorInput::new()`
returns `Err(KindMismatchError)`, the worker emits `CommitStageOutput::Failed` —
the same variant used for truly non-durable failures (findings submission errors,
done-ledger errors, etc.).

This is misleading because by the time this branch executes, the data is already
durably committed. Downstream retry logic seeing `Failed` could re-submit work
that already crossed the durability boundary.

In practice this path is unreachable: `expected_kind` is derived from the same
`completed_unit` that produces the receipt (the comment at line 459 says so).
But as defense-in-depth, the response to an "impossible" invariant violation
should be a hard failure signal, not a retryable-looking error.

## Current State

```rust
// crates/gossip-scanner-runtime/src/commit_pipeline.rs:453-466
let outcome = match result {
    Ok(receipt) => match CheckpointAggregatorInput::new(expected_kind, receipt) {
        Ok(checkpoint_input) => CommitStageOutput::Committed {
            write_context,
            checkpoint_input,
        },
        // Defense-in-depth: expected_kind was derived from the same
        // completed_unit that produced the receipt, so this kind check
        // cannot fail in practice.
        Err(kind_mismatch) => CommitStageOutput::Failed {
            write_context,
            completed_unit,
            error: ResultCommitError::KindMismatch(kind_mismatch),
        },
    },
    Err(error) => { ... }
};
```

`CommitStageOutput::Failed` conflates two semantically different failure modes:
1. Non-durable failures (findings/done-ledger errors) — retry makes sense
2. Post-durable invariant violations (KindMismatch) — retry is pointless; this is a bug

## Desired State

Replace the `CommitStageOutput::Failed` emission for `KindMismatch` with a
`debug_assert!` that catches the invariant violation in tests, and log an error
with a clear "data is durable but receipt is malformed" message. Since this path
is unreachable, the simplest correct response is:

```rust
Err(kind_mismatch) => {
    debug_assert!(
        false,
        "KindMismatch after successful commit is an internal invariant \
         violation: expected_kind was derived from the same completed_unit \
         that produced the receipt: {kind_mismatch}"
    );
    tracing::error!(
        %kind_mismatch,
        "commit-stage: internal invariant violation — data is durable but \
         receipt kind does not match completed unit (this should be unreachable)"
    );
    CommitStageOutput::Failed {
        write_context,
        completed_unit,
        error: ResultCommitError::KindMismatch(kind_mismatch),
    }
}
```

This preserves the existing `Failed` variant for release builds (no behavioral
change), but catches the invariant violation in debug/test builds and makes the
log message clearly distinguish this from retryable failures.

