## Design Notes

The biggest design choice here is whether `FilesystemConnector` should satisfy `OrderedContentSource` directly or via an adapter. The current codebase already has a generic runtime seam parameterized over `OrderedContentSource`, and this task is specifically about making the connector contract-complete before runtime wiring. Prefer a direct trait implementation that delegates to the same inherent methods so there is one filesystem code path to maintain.

This task should also preserve the bounded-memory filesystem direction from `gossip-8936`. Older `gossip-8936.*` tasks describe a streaming `enumerate_page` / `WalkState` design, but those file references no longer match HEAD. Treat those tasks as historical prior art, not as line-accurate code references. The key point for this task is architectural: do not satisfy the contract by reintroducing a temporary collect-all / sort-all legacy path that later work would need to delete.

On version semantics, filesystem pages should default to weak claims. Nothing in the current connector proves immutable content identity, so `VersionId::Strong` would overstate the guarantee. A metadata-based weak version token is sufficient for this task as long as the rule is explicit and regression-tested.

## Risk Analysis

- If this task invents a filesystem-only adapter instead of completing the connector surface itself, later runtime work will end up with two page-fill paths that can drift.
- If `fill_page(...)` leans on slice-only helpers by first rebuilding a full sorted filesystem index, it will violate the bounded-memory direction the task is explicitly trying to preserve.
- If `ItemRef` includes absolute root bytes or root-specific secrets, the connector leaks host-local path information and stops being a clean opaque read handle.
- If `fill_page(...)` emits unvalidated page shapes, later runtime work can silently accept out-of-order, duplicate, or out-of-range keys.
- If `fill_page(...)` ignores `Budgets`, runtime boundedness can still be bypassed through oversized pages even after page-fill exists.
- If `open()` continues to ignore budgets entirely, runtime boundedness can be bypassed through the full-read path even after page-fill exists.
- Reintroducing whole-tree materialization would conflict with the existing filesystem scalability direction under `gossip-8936` and create a second connector architecture that later work must unwind.

## Performance Considerations

This is a HOT/WARM boundary task: page fill is steady-state connector work, while `open()` is a per-item WARM read path. Keep key-ordering and cursor progression on the connector side allocation-conscious, preserve the existing single-entry read cache, and do not add repeated root canonicalization or root hashing per item. If `open()` becomes budget-aware, it should do so with a bounded reader or equivalent streaming guard rather than by buffering whole files.

