## Design Notes

- Keep `RepoKey` authoritative for outer ordering and `repo_id` authoritative only for inner persistence namespacing. Do not let two unrelated identity schemes drift apart.
- Ticket 2 should not absorb Ticket 3. It should provide the low-level identity helper and duplicate-identity check that the later Git request model will call, not the full user-facing request surface.
- Prefer a versioned, domain-separated identity helper so future encoding changes can be deliberate instead of silently mutating persisted namespaces.
- Keep selection out of `RepoKey`; selection-aware collision handling belongs to Ticket 3, while this ticket only rejects duplicate normalized repo identities.

## Risk Analysis

| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |
|------|-----------|--------|------------|
| Canonical identity is encoded one way in `RepoKey` and another way in `repo_id` derivation | Medium | High | Centralize both behind one normalized identity helper and test them together. |
| Raw repo paths leak through debug output or errors | Medium | Medium | Reuse `RepoLocator`/`ToxicDigest` redaction patterns and add regression coverage. |
| Ticket 2 grows into Ticket 3 and duplicates request-model work | Medium | Medium | Keep this task focused on identity primitives and helpers; defer the full request model to Ticket 3 explicitly. |
| A future encoding change silently invalidates persisted namespaces | Low | High | Use a versioned, domain-separated identity helper and add golden/round-trip tests. |
| `repo_id` collisions cause namespace aliasing | Low | Medium | Keep `RepoKey` as the authoritative outer identity and derive `repo_id` with the existing BLAKE3 identity utilities rather than hand-rolled hashes. |

## Performance Considerations

This work is COLD/WARM control-plane and persistence-namespace plumbing, not a HOT loop rewrite. The important performance constraints are:
- request normalization should remain `O(R log R)` for `R` repo targets because sorting/dedup happens once per submission,
- `repo_id` derivation should happen once per normalized repo target and reuse canonical identity bytes rather than re-hashing raw paths at multiple layers,
