## Context

The seen-blob store tracks which blob OIDs have already been scanned across scan runs, enabling idempotent crash recovery. Today it writes one RocksDB key per blob OID, with a ~63-byte key and a 1-byte presence marker. For a large repository with 10 million unique blobs this consumes ~750MB of RocksDB space, suffers 33x LSM write amplification from the pathological 43:1 key-to-value ratio, and requires N `multi_get` point lookups per batch check.

Deep research across 5 independent agents (foundational theory, production systems, failure modes, Rust ecosystem, industry practice) converged on a single recommendation: replace the per-OID RocksDB keys with a single serialized Roaring bitmap value per `(repo_id, policy_hash)` scope. This yields ~100-200x space reduction while preserving exact membership (no false positives), crash safety via `WriteBatch`, and compatibility with the existing `SeenBlobStore` trait.

The research confirmed:
- Per-ref watermarks and commit-graph range walking are already near-optimal (Kafka, Flink, CDC, Git all use the same pattern). No change needed there.
- Bloom filters are unacceptable as sole truth for a security scanner (1% FPR at 10M blobs = 100K silently skipped blobs).
- The `roaring` Rust crate (910 stars, Meilisearch/Kerollmops) is production-proven in Lucene, Druid, ClickHouse, and Sourcegraph.
- The codebase already has position-indexed bitmap infrastructure (`DynamicBitSet`, `AtomicBitSet`, `AtomicSeenSets`) for within-run dedup. The gap is cross-run bitmap persistence.
- A sorted OID array provides a repack-resilient OID-to-position mapping (unlike MIDX positions which change on `git gc`).

