## Context

When loading watermarks during `init_next_ref()`, the system must validate that the watermark OID is an ancestor of the current tip before trusting it for incremental scanning. Today this requires an `is_ancestor()` DFS walk through the commit graph. For deep histories (monorepos with millions of commits), this walk is expensive and runs for every ref on every scan invocation — even though force-pushes are rare.

By storing generation numbers alongside watermark OIDs, the system can detect force-pushes (or rebases) in O(1): if the generation of the resolved watermark position differs from the stored generation, the ref was rewritten. This eliminates the DFS for the common case (no force-push) and immediately detects mismatches for the uncommon case.

This is sub-task 4 from the parent epic gossip-9483 (mid-scan resumption and checkpoint recovery).

