## Context

For repos with 10M+ commits, the BFS loading in `CommitGraphMem::build()` is O(N+E) where N=commits and E=parent edges. This can take tens of seconds just for commit graph construction, before any actual scanning begins. On repeat scans of the same large repo, this startup cost is entirely wasted because the on-disk commit-graph file already contains the same data in a memory-mappable format.

`gix_commitgraph::Graph` (already a dependency at v0.34) can read the on-disk commit-graph directly via mmap, providing O(1) startup time. The `CommitGraph` trait at `crates/scanner-git/src/commit_walk.rs:78-106` already defines a clean abstraction boundary — the commit walk, commit planning, and tree diffing code all operate through this trait, not through `CommitGraphMem` directly. Adding a second implementation for `gix_commitgraph::Graph` would be transparent to all downstream consumers.

This task is about reading for speed, not about persisting positions. OID watermarks remain the durable frontier. The `CommitGraph::lookup()` method works the same way regardless of the backing implementation; the only difference is startup time.

## Current State

`acquire_commit_graph()` in `crates/scanner-git/src/artifact_acquire.rs:170-204` always builds `CommitGraphMem` from BFS-loaded commits, regardless of whether a valid on-disk commit-graph file exists:

```rust
// crates/scanner-git/src/artifact_acquire.rs:170-204
pub fn acquire_commit_graph(
    repo: &RepoJobState,
    midx: &MidxView<'_>,
    pack_paths: &[PathBuf],
    limits: &ArtifactBuildLimits,
) -> Result<CommitGraphMem, ArtifactAcquireError> {
    let tips: Vec<OidBytes> = repo.start_set.iter().map(|r| r.tip).collect();
    // ... always does BFS loading via load_commits_from_tips() ...
    let commits = load_commits_from_tips(
        &tips, midx, pack_paths, &loose_dirs,
        &shallow_boundary_roots, repo.object_format, &limits.commit_load, None,
    )?;
    CommitGraphMem::build(commits, repo.object_format)
        .map_err(ArtifactAcquireError::CommitGraphBuild)
}
```

The BFS commit loader (`crates/scanner-git/src/commit_loader.rs:321-340`) uses `CommitLoader::load_from_tips()` which traverses all reachable commits, mmapping and decoding each pack object individually. For 10M commits this involves:
- ~10M hash insertions into `visited` and `queued` HashSets
- ~10M pack object lookups and zlib inflations
- O(N log N) sort by `(generation, oid)` in `CommitGraphMem::build()` (`commit_graph_mem.rs:200-204`)

The repo open stage already detects the on-disk commit-graph path (`crates/scanner-git/src/repo_open.rs:337-345`):

```rust
fn commit_graph_path(objects_dir: &Path) -> PathBuf {
    let info_dir = objects_dir.join("info");
    let split_chain = info_dir.join("commit-graphs").join("commit-graph-chain");
    if is_file(&split_chain) {
        split_chain
    } else {
        info_dir.join("commit-graph")
    }
}
```

But this path is only used for lock-file detection via `RepoArtifactPaths::commit_graph`, not for reading.

The `gix_commitgraph::Graph` API (v0.34) already provides all methods needed to implement the `CommitGraph` trait:
- `Graph::num_commits() -> u32`
- `Graph::lookup(id) -> Option<Position>`
- `Graph::commit_at(pos) -> Commit` with `.generation()`, `.root_tree_id()`, `.committer_timestamp()`, `.iter_parents()`
- `Graph::id_at(pos) -> &gix_hash::oid`
- `Graph::at(path)` / `Graph::from_info_dir(path)` for loading

There is no existing `impl CommitGraph for gix_commitgraph::Graph` in the codebase.

## Desired State

A threshold-gated fast path in `acquire_commit_graph()` that reads the on-disk commit-graph file via mmap when it exists, covers all start-set tips, and the repo exceeds a configurable commit count threshold. Small repos and repos without commit-graph files continue to use the BFS path.

Specifically:
1. A new `impl CommitGraph` for an on-disk graph adapter provides O(1) access via mmap.
2. `acquire_commit_graph()` tries the on-disk path first when conditions are met, falling back to BFS otherwise.
3. The artifact fingerprint includes the commit-graph chain file for change detection.
4. `ConcurrentMaintenance` detection extends to commit-graph file changes.

