## Context

Step 5 of the done-ledger Bloom filter epic (gossip-9550). This is the core integration: modify `prefilter_done_ledger()` to check the Bloom filter before issuing `batch_get()`. Items where the filter says "definitely not in the done ledger" are classified as `ScanMiss` immediately, bypassing the PostgreSQL round-trip. Items where the filter says "maybe present" still go through the normal `batch_get()` confirmation path.

The done-ledger monotonic lattice guarantee ensures correctness: filter negatives are authoritative (Bloom filters have zero false negatives), so an item the filter says is absent is genuinely absent from the filter's construction snapshot. False positives (filter says "maybe present" but DB says absent) cost one unnecessary DB lookup per false-positive item, bounded to ~1% by the FPR.

## Current State

`prefilter_done_ledger()` at `ordered_content.rs:333-408` processes items in chunks of `RECOMMENDED_MAX_BATCH_SIZE` (10,000):

```rust
// ordered_content.rs:333-408 — current flow
pub fn prefilter_done_ledger<D>(
    self,
    write_context: WriteContext,
    done_ledger: &D,
) -> Result<OrderedContentPrefilteredPage, ScanRuntimeError>
{
    // For each chunk:
    //   1. Derive OvidHash per item
    //   2. Call done_ledger.batch_get() — PostgreSQL round-trip
    //   3. Classify: terminal status → AlreadyDone, retryable/absent → ScanMiss
}
```

Every chunk triggers a `batch_get()` regardless of whether any items are likely to be in the done ledger.

## Desired State

Add an `Option<&DoneLedgerBloomFilter>` parameter. When `Some`, partition each chunk: items the filter says are definitely absent become `ScanMiss` immediately; only "maybe present" items go to `batch_get()`.

```rust
pub fn prefilter_done_ledger<D>(
    self,
    write_context: WriteContext,
    done_ledger: &D,
    bloom: Option<&DoneLedgerBloomFilter>,
) -> Result<OrderedContentPrefilteredPage, ScanRuntimeError>
{
    // For each chunk:
    //   1. Derive OvidHash per item (unchanged)
    //   2. NEW: If bloom is Some, partition into:
    //      - filter-negatives → classify as ScanMiss immediately
    //      - filter-positives → send to batch_get()
    //   3. If bloom is None, send all to batch_get() (existing behavior)
    //   4. Classify batch_get() results (unchanged)
}
```

When the filter is present and the done-ledger is mostly empty (first scan), most items are filter-negatives, and `batch_get()` receives a small or empty batch — potentially skipping the PostgreSQL round-trip entirely for that chunk.

