## Context

During Git repo-frontier scanning, `FindingsCaptureSink` captures two correlated data streams: findings (each with a `commit_id` ordinal) and commit OID mappings (`commit_id → OidBytes`). Post-scan, `translate_git_findings` joins them to derive per-object version identity. The OID map has a 500K entry cap as a memory safety bound. When saturated, new commit IDs are silently dropped while findings continue accumulating. This creates a consistency violation: findings reference commit IDs whose OID mappings were discarded, causing `MissingGitCommitOid` errors during persistence translation and aborting the shard after a full scan.

## Current State

`FindingsCaptureSink` (coordination_sink.rs:370-535) has `commit_oid_map: Mutex<HashMap<u32, OidBytes>>` with `MAX_COMMIT_OID_MAP_ENTRIES = 500_000`. In `emit_git` (line 511-535), when the map reaches capacity, new keys are silently dropped:

```rust
let below_cap = map.len() < Self::MAX_COMMIT_OID_MAP_ENTRIES;
if below_cap || map.contains_key(&meta.commit_id) {
    map.insert(meta.commit_id, meta.commit_oid);
    if below_cap && map.len() == Self::MAX_COMMIT_OID_MAP_ENTRIES {
        tracing::warn!(max = Self::MAX_COMMIT_OID_MAP_ENTRIES,
            "commit OID map reached capacity; new keys will be dropped");
    }
}
```

Meanwhile findings continue flowing into `captured_findings: Mutex<Vec<GitFindingForPersistence>>` with no cap. The `emit_git` interface returns `()` (fire-and-forget), so the sink cannot propagate errors.

Post-scan in `translate_git_findings` (result_translation.rs:695-697):
```rust
let commit_oid = commit_oid_map
    .get(&commit_id)
    .ok_or(ResultTranslationError::MissingGitCommitOid { index, commit_id })?;
```

This fails for every finding referencing a dropped commit ID, aborting the shard after all expensive scan work is complete. The repo gets re-claimed and re-scanned, hitting the same cap — creating an infinite retry loop.

## Desired State

When the OID map saturates, the scan is cancelled immediately via the existing `CancellationToken`, preventing wasted scan work and the infinite retry loop. A clear error message identifies OID map saturation as the cause.

