### Wire Format Change

Under the no-versioning policy, ALL existing checkpoints become invalid after this change. This is acceptable — stale checkpoints from before the change will fail the magic-byte check and be discarded via the existing `Ok(None)` graceful-restart path.

### Implementation Approach (enrichment)

#### Allocation Tier: COLD
All checkpoint encode/decode functions are called at most once per stage boundary (4 boundaries max per scan) or once at resume load. No allocation constraints — optimize for clarity.

#### CRC32 Envelope Layout
Use an 8-byte fixed header: `[magic:4][crc32_le:4][payload...]`. No length prefix needed — the storage backend already knows blob length. `crc32fast` uses hardware acceleration (SSE4.2/NEON) when available.

```rust
const CHECKPOINT_MAGIC: [u8; 4] = *b"gkpt";
const ENVELOPE_HEADER_LEN: usize = 8; // magic(4) + crc32(4)

fn checkpoint_serialize<T: serde::Serialize>(value: &T) -> Result<Vec<u8>, ScanCheckpointError> {
    let payload = postcard::to_allocvec(value)
        .map_err(|err| ScanCheckpointError::Encode(err.to_string()))?;
    if payload.len() > MAX_CHECKPOINT_BYTES {
        tracing::error!(
            size = payload.len(),
            limit = MAX_CHECKPOINT_BYTES,
            "checkpoint blob exceeds decode-side size limit; \
             the blob will be persisted but discarded on the next resume"
        );
    }
    let crc = crc32fast::hash(&payload);
    let mut envelope = Vec::with_capacity(ENVELOPE_HEADER_LEN + payload.len());
    envelope.extend_from_slice(&CHECKPOINT_MAGIC);
    envelope.extend_from_slice(&crc.to_le_bytes());
    envelope.extend_from_slice(&payload);
    Ok(envelope)
}
```

#### Return Type Change
`checkpoint_deserialize` must return `Result<T, ScanCheckpointError>` instead of `Result<T, postcard::Error>`. The function now has non-postcard failure modes (magic, CRC, size). Callers in `from_loaded` already handle `ScanCheckpointError` — simplify the error conversion.

#### Verification Order (fail-fast)
`checkpoint_deserialize` verification order: **magic → size → CRC → postcard**. This ensures:
- Magic check runs BEFORE CRC computation (avoid computing CRC on non-checkpoint data)
- Size guard runs BEFORE CRC computation (avoid computing CRC on 256 MiB+ blob that will be rejected)

```rust
fn checkpoint_deserialize<'de, T: serde::Deserialize<'de>>(
    bytes: &'de [u8],
) -> Result<T, ScanCheckpointError> {
    if bytes.len() < ENVELOPE_HEADER_LEN {
        return Err(ScanCheckpointError::Decode("blob too short for envelope header".into()));
    }
    if bytes[..4] != CHECKPOINT_MAGIC {
        return Err(ScanCheckpointError::Decode("invalid checkpoint magic bytes".into()));
    }
    let stored_crc = u32::from_le_bytes(bytes[4..8].try_into().unwrap());
    let payload = &bytes[ENVELOPE_HEADER_LEN..];
    if payload.len() > MAX_CHECKPOINT_BYTES {
        return Err(ScanCheckpointError::Decode("payload exceeds size limit".into()));
    }
    let computed_crc = crc32fast::hash(payload);
    if stored_crc != computed_crc {
        return Err(ScanCheckpointError::Decode(
            format!("CRC32 mismatch: stored={stored_crc:#010x}, computed={computed_crc:#010x}")
        ));
    }
    postcard::from_bytes(payload).map_err(|e| ScanCheckpointError::Decode(e.to_string()))
}
```

#### `completed_plan_count` Validation Analysis
Investigation result: `packed.len()` IS a valid upper bound because pack plans are created by bucketing packed candidates by `pack_id`, so `plans.len() <= unique_pack_ids(packed) <= packed.len()`. The bound is loose but correct. A tighter check would require O(n) unique pack_id scan, which is unnecessary complexity. The right fix is to rename `plan_len` to something clearer and add a comment explaining why `packed.len()` is the correct conservative bound.

